                tatami - package manager built on the pkgutils design


Description
-----------
tatami keeps the architecture of pkgutils (CRUX): a small C++ core on top of
libarchive, a plain-text package database with a crash-safe commit, regex
UPGRADE/INSTALL rules and .old copies of replaced configuration files,
and a bash tool (tatamk) that builds packages from recipes. On top of that it
has an apt-style command line and uses libsolv to resolve dependencies.


Building and installing
-----------------------
Needs a C++11 compiler, a C compiler and cmake. Nothing else is taken from the
system: libsolv, libarchive, zstd and zlib are vendored as git submodules in
deps/ and built as static archives, which are linked into the tatami binary
by path (no pkg-config, no -l flags). This makes the build identical on every
distribution; some ship no libsolv.a, and libarchive/zstd/zlib versions and
feature sets differ. After a fresh clone fetch the sources once:

$ git submodule update --init

$ make
$ make install                      # or: make DESTDIR=/some/other/path install
$ make PKG_EXT=myext                # build with your own package extension
$ make STATIC=yes                   # fully static binary
$ make check                        # end-to-end tests (needs root)
$ make clean                        # keeps the built libraries
$ make distclean                    # also removes the deps/*/cmake-build directories

Pinned versions, all release tags: libarchive v3.8.9, zstd v1.5.7, zlib v1.3.2,
xz v5.8.4, bzip2 1.0.8, lz4 v1.10.0, libsodium 1.0.22-RELEASE, libsolv 0.7.40 (see
'git submodule status'). To update one, check out the new tag inside its deps/
directory, remove its deps/<name>/cmake-build directory so it is rebuilt, and commit
the new submodule revision. Each static archive is only built when it does not exist
yet. libsodium is the one autotools project: it is configured from its shipped
configure script (a POSIX shell and make are enough) in deps/libsodium/cmake-build,
a directory named like the others so that clean-up is uniform.

libarchive is built with zlib, zstd, liblzma, bzip2 and lz4, and without openssl, acl,
xattr or libxml. Every compression format is read and written by tatami itself; no
host program is needed for any of them.

Build-time configuration: settings come from the make command line, a config.mk
next to the Makefile (copy config.mk.example), the environment, then defaults.
  PREFIX (/usr), BINDIR, MANDIR, DESTDIR   where 'make install' puts things
  SYSCONFDIR (/etc)                        <root>/etc/tatami/{tatami.conf,repos.conf,hooks.d}
  DB_DIR (/var/lib/tatami)                 database, journal, metadata, repos, default repository
  CACHEDIR (/var/cache/tatami)             downloaded packages
  PKG_EXT                                  a fixed package extension
SYSCONFDIR, DB_DIR and CACHEDIR are compiled in (TATAMI_SYSCONFDIR, TATAMI_DB_DIR,
TATAMI_CACHEDIR) and are always understood below --root, so
'make SYSCONFDIR=/opt/etc' makes tatami read <root>/opt/etc/tatami/tatami.conf.
tatamk gets SYSCONFDIR too. 'make clean' removes objects and binaries and keeps the
vendored libraries; 'make distclean' removes those as well. config.mk is never touched.


Command line
------------
tatami [options] <command> [arguments]

  index <dir>...         write the binary repo.solv index of repository directories
  update                 do that for every configured repository
  install <pkg|file>...  install packages and their dependencies; installing
                         an installed package upgrades it. "name=version"
                         pins a version, a file argument installs that file
  remove <package>...    remove packages, what depends on them, and the dependencies
                         that are no longer needed (always; there is no autoremove)
  upgrade                upgrade all installed packages
  search <regex>         search the repository index (names and descriptions)
  show <package>...      show package details
  list [regex]           list INSTALLED packages (read from the database only);
                         --available lists the repository index instead,
                         --upgradable the installed ones with a newer version
  files <package|file>   list the files of a package
  owns <regex>           find the package owning a file

Options: -y (no questions), -s (simulate), -f (overwrite conflicting files),
-r <root> (default /), -c <config file>.

Also used by tatamk, not meant to be run by hand: 'tatami pack' (create a package
archive in any of the supported compressions) and 'tatami bulkhead' (the sandbox).

Everything operates below the root given with -r, so a package set can be
tried out in a scratch directory.


Installation reasons and removal
--------------------------------
tatami records why each package is installed: "explicit" if you asked for it,
"dependency" if it was only pulled in to satisfy the dependencies of other
packages. The reason is stored in the package's metadata below
/var/lib/tatami/meta and shown by 'tatami show' (a package cannot choose its
own reason in its .META). Installing an already installed dependency by name
makes it explicit; upgrades keep the reason. Packages without a recorded reason
(installed before this was tracked) count as explicit and are never removed
automatically.

'tatami remove X' always cleans up, so no cruft is left behind: it removes X, the
packages that depend on X, and every dependency that no explicitly installed
package needs any more. The orphan calculation is libsolv's own: the explicit
packages are passed to it as SOLVER_USERINSTALLED and the removal is a
SOLVER_ERASE with SOLVER_CLEANDEPS. The plan shows each package that goes only
because it is no longer needed. There is deliberately no separate autoremove
command. 'tatami -s remove X' shows the result without changing anything.


Atomic transactions and rollback
--------------------------------
Everything one command does (all the installs, upgrades and removals of its plan)
is one transaction: all or nothing. Every change the extractions make is recorded
first: files and directories they create (including the parent directories
libarchive makes on the way), files they overwrite (moved aside in their own
directory, so on the same filesystem), and the mode and owner of existing
directories they touch. Removals and the files an upgrade drops are not deleted
yet, and the package database is only changed in memory.

Only when every package is on disk is the transaction committed: the deletions are
journalled, the database is written (one atomic rename, the commit point), and
then the old files and backups go. The first error of any kind in any package (a
file that cannot be written, a full disk, a corrupt or truncated archive, a
conflict, ...) stops the run, deletes what was created (bottom-up), restores what
was overwritten, and leaves the database untouched: the packages that had already
been extracted are undone too. A file that a new package takes over from a package
being removed in the same run is kept, with the new content.

Configuration files (the files an UPGRADE rule in tatami.conf says NO to, by default
under etc/) are handled Slackware-style: when an upgrade brings a version that differs
from the file on disk, the new one is installed and the existing one is renamed to
<file>.old (an identical file is left alone, an earlier .old is replaced). The rename is
journalled like every other change ("O"): a rollback, or the recovery of a killed run,
deletes the new file and renames the .old back; after a commit the .old stays.

The record is also kept in var/lib/tatami/transaction.journal ("T" starts a run,
"I"/"R" note the packages, "K" is written right before the database commit). If
tatami is killed (SIGKILL) or the machine loses power in the middle of a run, the
next command that modifies the system reads the journal first: without a "K" the
database cannot have been written and the whole run is rolled back; with one, the
database decides: if it shows the result of every operation the run is finished
(deferred deletions, backups), otherwise it is rolled back. It says which.
For testing, TATAMI_CRASH_AT=extracted|committed makes tatami kill itself at
those two points.

Two things happen without any option. Before anything is extracted tatami adds up the
unpacked size of all incoming packages (plus about half a block per file and a margin
of 5% and 8 MiB) and compares it with the free space of the root's filesystem
(statvfs); if it does not fit the run is refused with the numbers and nothing is
touched. And once a transaction has committed, the packages it downloaded are deleted
from var/cache/tatami/packages together with their .sig files; after a rollback they
stay, so that a retry does not download them again.


Package format and names
------------------------
A package is a tar archive compressed with gzip, zstd, xz/lzma, bzip2 or lz4
(or not at all). All of these are read and written by tatami itself: libarchive
is built with zlib, zstd, liblzma, bzip2 and lz4, all vendored in deps/, so no
tar, xz, bzip2 or other program on the host is involved (tatamk packs through
'tatami pack'; unpacking the *source* tarballs of a recipe still uses the host's
tar, since upstream sources can be anything).

The file name is   <name>_<version>-<release>_<arch>.tar.<compression>
for example        zlib_1.3.2-1_x86_64.tar.zst
                   linux-api-headers_6.6-1_aarch64.tar.xz
                   tzdata_2026a-1_noarch.tar.gz
Name and version may not contain '_' (hyphens are fine), so the first two
underscores are the separators and the architecture, which may contain one
(x86_64), is everything after them. Inside the archive a metadata member named
.META is not installed. It uses "key: value" lines:

  name: example
  version: 1.0-1
  arch: x86_64               (noarch/all/any: installable everywhere)
  description: Example tool
  depends: libfoo, libbar >= 2.0, a | b
  conflicts: example-git
  provides: example-virtual
  replaces: oldexample

depends/conflicts use libsolv relations: name, "name >= version" (also <, <=,
=, >), and "a | b" alternatives. "replaces" removes the named package (or what
provides that name) when this one is installed.


Custom package extension" below). tatami and tatamk both use it,
so "tar.pkg", "tatami" are all fine. Files that do not end in
the configured extension are not indexed.

The archive contains the files of the package and a metadata member named
.META, which is not installed. It uses "key: value" lines:

  name: example
  version: 1.0-1
  arch: x86_64               (noarch/all/any: installable everywhere)
  description: Example tool
  depends: libfoo, libbar >= 2.0, a | b
  conflicts: example-git
  provides: example-virtual
  replaces: oldexample

depends/conflicts use libsolv relations: name, "name >= version" (also <, <=,
=, >), and "a | b" alternatives. "replaces" removes the named package when
this one is installed.


Custom package extension
------------------------
By default the extension is tar.<compression> and tatami accepts every one. Anyone
building their own distribution can fix a single extension instead, no source
changes needed:

$ make PKG_EXT=myext

The one variable is used everywhere: tatami is compiled with
-DDEFAULT_PKG_EXT="myext" (it is what indexing scans for and what a file argument
is recognised by) and tatamk is generated from tatamk.in with @DEFAULT_PKG_EXT@
replaced by "myext" (it names the packages it builds that way, <name>_<version>_<arch>.myext).
Left empty, as by default, the names are tar.<compression>. The value may contain
letters, digits, '.', '_' and '-' and no leading dot.

The built-in value can be overridden when running, in this order of precedence:
  1. PKG_EXT in the environment (tatamk only)
  2. PKG_EXT in /etc/tatami/tatami.conf (tatami and tatamk)
  3. the build-time default
Keep tatami and tatamk on the same extension: if you override it only for
tatamk through the environment, set the same PKG_EXT in tatami.conf or tatami
will not see the packages.


Recipes (Tatamifile)
--------------------
Same idea as pkgmk's Pkgfile: a shell script. Metadata are variables, the
build is a function. $SRC is the unpacked source, $PKG the package root.

  # Description: Example tool
  name=example
  version=1.0
  release=1
  source=(http://host/example-$version.tar.gz)
  depends=(libfoo "libbar >= 2.0")
  conflicts=()  provides=()  replaces=()

  build() {
          cd example-$version
          ./configure --prefix=/usr
          make
          make DESTDIR=$PKG install
  }

tatamk [-f recipe] [-o outdir] builds it into <name>_<version>-<release>.<ext>.
A ".md5sum" file next to the recipe is checked if present.


Isolated builds (bulkhead)
--------------------------
tatamk runs build() inside a Bubblewrap sandbox by default, through

  tatami bulkhead [--ro <path>]... <host_destdir> -- <command> [args...]

which runs  bwrap --ro-bind / / --dev /dev --proc /proc --tmpfs /tmp
--tmpfs /home --tmpfs /root [--ro-bind <path> <path>]...
--bind <host_destdir> /tmp/destdir --unshare-net --unshare-pid --die-with-parent
-- <command>  and passes the command's exit status through (128+N when it
was killed by signal N; 255 if bwrap could not be started; 2 for usage errors).
Inside, the system is read-only and offline, the host's processes are not visible
(own PID namespace and /proc), /home and /root are empty (tatamk maps the recipe
and the sources back read-only with --ro, since they may live there), $SRC is a copy in /tmp/build/src and
$PKG and $DESTDIR are /tmp/destdir (inside the tmpfs, so no mount point is needed
on the read-only root). Sources are fetched and checked before the
sandbox starts, so downloads must be declared in source=().

tatamk refuses to build when bwrap is missing, unless you opt out with
--no-bulkhead (alias --no-sandbox) or BULKHEAD=no in tatami.conf. --bulkhead
forces the sandbox over the config setting.


Repositories and the repo.solv index
------------------------------------
A repository is a directory of package files, local or remote. They are listed
as name=url lines in <root>/etc/tatami/repos.conf and <root>/etc/tatami/repos.d/*.conf:

  local=/mnt/repo
  usb=file:///media/usb/packages
  core=https://my-os.example/core

(REPO_DIRS in tatami.conf still works when there is no repos.conf; the first
repository listed wins when a version is in several.) If nothing is configured the
repository is the local directory <root>/var/lib/tatami/packages, so tatami is
offline unless you explicitly add an http://, https:// or ftp:// repository.
Dependencies are resolved across all repositories together.

$ tatami index /srv/packages          writes /srv/packages/repo.solv (the server side)
$ tatami update                       indexes local repositories, fetches remote ones

repo.solv is libsolv's own binary repository format: it holds each package's
name, version, architecture, dependencies, conflicts, provides, replaces, size and
file name. A local directory that has one is read through it. If packages were
added after it was written, tatami says so and scans the directory instead (so the
result is never wrong, just slower) until you index again.

Remote repositories: tatami has no network code. 'tatami update' runs FETCHCMD
(tatami.conf; default: curl -f -L -# "%URL%" -o "%DEST%") to download <url>/repo.solv
to <root>/var/lib/tatami/repos/<name>/repo.solv; a download that is not a valid
index is discarded and the previous one kept. 'tatami install' loads those cached
indexes and, for a package from a remote repository, runs FETCHCMD for
<url>/<file> into <root>/var/cache/tatami/packages/, checks that it holds the
package the index promised, and installs it from there (a cached file is reused).
FETCHCMD is run directly, not through a shell, so nothing in a URL is interpreted.
Its stderr is read through a pipe: when stdout is a terminal tatami draws its own
progress bar from the percentages that curl -# prints and hides the raw output
(without a terminal, or for a command that prints no percentages, nothing is drawn).
If the command fails, its last message is shown in the error. A local path or
file:// repository never starts FETCHCMD. Publish a remote repository by putting
the packages and the repo.solv of 'tatami index' on a web server.


Holding packages
----------------
HoldPkg = name1 name2 in tatami.conf (space-separated, may be repeated) pins installed
packages: each gets a SOLVER_LOCK job, so libsolv leaves them exactly as they are
whatever else is upgraded or removed ('tatami upgrade' reports each one that has a newer
version as "Skipping upgrade for held package: name (1.0 -> 2.0)"). A package named in
the install or remove command itself is not held for that command: an explicit
request wins. Held packages that are not installed are ignored.


Signing packages
----------------
Ed25519 (RFC 8032, pre-hash variant) through libsodium (deps/libsodium, linked statically; no OpenSSL). tatami has two small commands for it:

$ tatami keygen                     private key ~/.tatami/privkey (0600), public key
                                    ~/.tatami/privkey.pub, prints the PUBKEY line
$ tatami sign <file> [keyfile]      writes <file>.sig

tatamk signs as part of the build: if $TATAMI_PRIVKEY or ~/.tatami/privkey exists, it
refreshes the repo.solv of the output directory and signs both the package and the
repo.solv (--no-sign skips it; without a key it builds unsigned, as before). Publish
the packages together with their .sig files and repo.solv(.sig). On the installing
machines set PUBKEY = <public key> in tatami.conf. Then, for remote repositories only:

  - 'tatami update' fetches repo.solv and repo.solv.sig and verifies them before
    anything is stored; 'tatami install' does the same for each package, before it
    is stored in the cache or installed. A bad, wrong-key or missing signature aborts.
  - cached indexes are verified again each time they are loaded (one that fails is
    ignored with a warning); a cached package is only reused if its signature verifies.
  - without a PUBKEY a remote repository is refused. PUBKEY = none turns the check off.

Package checksums: 'tatami index' (and so tatamk) stores the SHA-256 of every package
file in repo.solv (libsolv's SOLVABLE_CHECKSUM; 'tatami show' prints it), so the signed
index binds the packages it lists and a file swapped on the server is caught even
without signatures. A package downloaded from a remote repository is hashed (libsodium)
right after the download and must match; if not it is deleted and the run is
refused. A cached copy must match as well, and an index that has no checksum for a
remote package is refused.

Local sources are trusted: a package file given on the command line, a file:// or
plain path repository, are never checked (neither signature nor checksum) and need no key. The signature covers a file
byte for byte; it does not hide anything, and a key you lose cannot be recovered.


Target root (--root)
--------------------
Every command takes -r <root> (default /). Everything tatami reads or writes
belongs to that root and nothing outside it is touched:

  <root>/var/lib/tatami/db                  the package database
  <root>/var/lib/tatami/                   journal, install reasons
  <root>/etc/tatami/tatami.conf                   configuration
  <root>/etc/tatami/hooks.d/               hooks
  the files of the packages

Symbolic links inside the root are followed as in a chroot: in a usr-merged root
with /lib -> usr/lib, or even /lib -> /usr/lib (an absolute link), a package's
lib/libfoo.so lands in <root>/usr/lib, never in the host's /usr/lib; ".." cannot
climb out; removing the package removes the root's file, not the host's. A
directory a package lists (lib/) that is a symbolic link in the root belongs to
the base system and is never removed with the package. A symlink loop is an
error. (Repository directories and the programs a hook runs are host paths: they
are inputs, not part of the root.)


Target architecture
-------------------
libsolv's pool is set to the target's architecture, ARCH in <root>/etc/tatami/tatami.conf
(default: the machine's). Only that architecture and noarch packages can be
installed or serve as dependencies; others are refused with "built for
architecture X, which is incompatible with this system (Y)". ARCH=x86_64:i686
allows both for a multilib system. tatamk puts the architecture (the recipe's
arch=, else the first ARCH entry, else uname -m) into .META and the file name.


Hooks
-----
What happens after a transaction is not built into tatami. A hook is a file
<root>/etc/tatami/hooks.d/NAME.hook:

  # rebuild the library cache when libraries changed
  Trigger   = ^usr/lib/              POSIX extended regex on the changed paths
  Trigger   = ^etc/ld\.so\.conf        (relative to the root, directories end in '/')
  Operation = install upgrade remove optional, default all
  Needs     = etc/ld.so.conf         optional: skipped unless this exists in the root
  Exec      = /sbin/ldconfig -r %ROOT%    quotes work, no shell; %ROOT% is the root

A hook runs once per transaction, after the database commit, if the transaction
added, replaced or removed a path that matches a Trigger. TATAMI_ROOT is set in
its environment. A failing hook is reported but does not undo anything. Hook files
are checked before the transaction starts, so a malformed one changes nothing.
'make install' installs hooks/ldconfig.hook, the example above.


Files
-----
/etc/tatami/tatami.conf               settings and UPGRADE/INSTALL rules
/var/lib/tatami/db              installed packages and their files
/var/lib/tatami/meta/<name>    metadata of installed packages, install reason
/var/lib/tatami/transaction.journal   undo log of an installation in progress
<repository>/repo.solv         binary index of a repository
/etc/tatami/repos.conf, repos.d/*.conf   repositories (name=url)
/var/lib/tatami/repos/<name>/  fetched indexes of remote repositories
/var/cache/tatami/packages/    downloaded packages
/etc/tatami/hooks.d/*.hook     post-transaction hooks


